# Tenant wiring

Each external repo that deploys into this cluster gets one YAML file here plus a
matching entry in `kustomization.yaml` (Kustomize requires explicit resource
listings). The file carries the tenant's secret store, Flux `GitRepository`,
reconciliation `ServiceAccount` + RoleBindings, and top-level `Kustomization`.

Removal is the inverse: delete the file and its `kustomization.yaml` line. Flux
prunes the Kustomization (and everything it created, including ESO-owned
Secrets), the GitRepository and the secret store — in that order.
`creationPolicy: Owner` puts an ownerReference from each ExternalSecret onto its
Secret, so the Secrets go with the ExternalSecrets; deleting a store on its own
removes nothing and only pushes its ExternalSecrets into `SecretSyncError`.

Every tenant's Namespace, ResourceQuota and LimitRange carry
`kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled` and outlive the wiring, database
or not; `scripts/check-tenant-wiring.py` refuses a tenant file whose Namespace
or either cap lacks the annotation. A pruned
Namespace cascades through every tenant object and PVC in it, and
`deletionPolicy: Orphan` on a db Kustomization cannot protect against that.
Delete the Namespace by hand once nothing in it is wanted.
{%- if postgres_operator %}

A tenant that runs a database comes down in this order, one step per change.
Remove the wiring from Git first: the app Kustomization, so nothing writes; then
the db Kustomization, which orphans the Cluster, its policies and its backups in
place; then the rest (this directory's tenant file and its `kustomization.yaml`
line, as above). Confirm the last base backup and WAL archive in the object
store, delete the CNPG `Cluster` by hand, and delete the Namespace by hand last:
while the tenant file that declares it is in Git, Flux recreates it.
`scripts/check-tenant-wiring.py` refuses a db Kustomization (named `*-db`, or
reconciling `./kubernetes/db`) that does not set `deletionPolicy: Orphan`.
{%- endif %}

## The tenant side of the contract

A tenant repo needs a `kubernetes/flux/` directory that `kustomize build`
resolves cleanly, and CI that lints it.
**[weisssrv-app-template](https://github.com/ericsweiss/weisssrv-app-template)**
generates exactly that — manifests, lint + kubeconform + secret-scan pipeline,
and the namespace/PSS conventions this cluster expects. Point new tenants at it
rather than at a hand-rolled tree.

Two things the tenant must match, and one this repo owes them:

- **Path.** The `Kustomization.spec.path` below is `./kubernetes/flux`; the
  app-template puts manifests there. Change one and change the other.
- **Namespace.** The tenant's manifests must be namespace-agnostic (the
  `targetNamespace` on the Kustomization sets it) or hard-code the namespace this
  file creates. Mixing the two produces resources in two namespaces, only one of
  which Flux prunes.
- **Deploys happen cluster-side.** Tenant CI lints and builds; it does not
  `kubectl apply`. Flux pulls. A tenant that also deploys from CI will fight the
  reconciler.
- **Substitution is strict.** Every `${...}` a tenant manifest carries must
  resolve from `cluster-config` or `cluster-versions`, or the tenant's whole
  reconcile fails. Escape a literal as `$${...}`.

Nothing about tenant onboarding requires the app-template — a hand-authored
`kubernetes/flux/` works identically. It just means writing the pipeline and the
conventions again, including the one the cluster mandates: a namespace-wide
ingress default-deny NetworkPolicy plus a scrape-allow from `observability`. The
platform's default-deny coverage gate reads only this repository, so a tenant
namespace is invisible to it.

### What the platform provides

Four cluster-side behaviours a tenant manifest binds to, none of which fail
loudly when they are missing:

- **The `infrastructure-configs` Flux Kustomization exists under that name.**
  Every tenant Kustomization names it in `dependsOn`, so a cluster that renames
  or drops the stage leaves each tenant retrying "dependency not found" forever.
  With `wait: true` nothing is applied and no tenant-side alert fires.
- **Prometheus discovers rules and monitors in every namespace.** The
  kube-prometheus-stack release sets `ruleSelectorNilUsesHelmValues: false`,
  `serviceMonitorSelectorNilUsesHelmValues: false` and the matching
  `*NamespaceSelector: {}`, so a tenant's `PrometheusRule` and `ServiceMonitor`
  are selected without a release label. A cluster left on the chart defaults
  ignores both without a word.
- **The Traefik `websecure` entryPoint.** Every tenant IngressRoute names it.
- **external-dns watches the Traefik source** with the
  `external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io` annotation prefix, pinned in
  `infrastructure/controllers/external-dns/release.yaml`. Under a different
  prefix the annotation is ignored and no record appears, while the pods stay
  ready and nothing alerts.

{% if tenant_storage -%}
### Persistent storage

StorageClass `tenant-local` serves tenant claims: one `local` volume per
claim, a directory on the NAS-colocated agent's `tenant-volumes` zvol, kept
(`Retain`) when the claim is deleted and never grown. It is not the default
class, so a claim must name it. A claim's size is accounting metadata: the
volume is a plain directory, and nothing stops it writing past its request. A
ResourceQuota on `tenant-local.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage`
bounds what each tenant namespace declares, not what it writes; the zvol's size
bounds what all tenant volumes together can fill, and `TenantVolumesFilling`
warns before they do. Until a backend that enforces sizes replaces this one,
the class is for trusted tenants only.

The provisioner's RBAC is upstream's with one deliberate deviation: `pods`
and `pods/log` are granted by a Role in `local-path-storage`, where its helper
pods run, not by the ClusterRole, so it cannot read pods or logs in a tenant
namespace. Nodes, claims, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, ConfigMaps and
events stay cluster-wide, as upstream needs. Re-apply the narrowing when
upgrading from upstream's `local-path-storage.yaml`.

{% endif -%}
{% if postgres_operator -%}
### PostgreSQL

The CloudNativePG operator and its Barman Cloud plugin run in `cnpg-system`,
and `tenant-crd-editor` grants the `postgresql.cnpg.io` and
`barmancloud.cnpg.io` kinds. Instance volumes come from the `tenant-local`
StorageClass above. Instance pods must admit `cnpg-system` on 8000,
`observability` on 9187 and their own application pods on 5432, and reach DNS,
the apiserver at `${cluster_apiserver_egress_cidr}` on 6443 and public 443 for
the object store. The pattern and the recovery drill are in docs/postgres.md.

{% endif -%}
### Tenant CI is the tenant's choice, and one option is none

**The cluster side is indifferent to a tenant's CI** — the deploy is always this
cluster pulling, never the tenant pushing.

The app-template asks for the shape up front — its `ci_shape` answer — so a
tenant is generated with the pipeline it wants rather than editing one out
afterwards:

| `ci_shape` | What the tenant repo carries |
|---|---|
| `gitlab_selfhosted` | a `.gitlab-ci.yml` that lints and kubeconforms `kubernetes/flux/` |
| `github` | the same gates as GitHub Actions workflows |
| `none` | no pipeline at all |

`none` is a supported shape, not a degraded one — the `Kustomization` below is
the only thing that applies anything — but it leaves no pre-merge gate and rests
entirely on Flux being able to clone the repository. Read "Private tenant
repositories" below before choosing it for a private tenant.

### Tenant repositories are host-independent

`{{ git_host }}` is where **this cluster's** repository lives. It has nothing to
do with where a tenant's repository lives: Flux clones over plain HTTPS or SSH,
so a tenant can sit on GitHub while this cluster sits on
`{{ git_host }}`, or on a third host entirely. Copy the example's URL only if the
tenant really is on the same host — otherwise write the tenant's own:

```yaml
# same host as this cluster
url: https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/example-app
# a GitHub-hosted tenant, on the same cluster
url: https://github.com/<owner>/example-app
# either host, over SSH (required for a private repo authenticated by deploy key)
url: ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/example-app.git
```

### Private tenant repositories

Flux authenticates per `GitRepository`, through a Secret named by
`spec.secretRef.name`. That Secret must live in the **same namespace as the
GitRepository** (`flux-system` here — `secretRef` is namespace-local), and it is
**bootstrap state, not Flux-managed**: it is what Flux needs in order to read
git, so it cannot itself come from git.

Deploy key (preferred — scoped to one repository, and revocable there):

```bash
flux create secret git example-app-git-auth \
  --namespace=flux-system \
  --url=ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/example-app \
  --ssh-key-algorithm=ecdsa \
  --ssh-ecdsa-curve=p521
```

That prints a public key. Add it to the tenant repository as a deploy key —
**read-only**, no write access; Flux never pushes to a tenant repo — then point
the source at it:

```yaml
spec:
  url: ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/example-app.git   # ssh://, and the .git suffix
  secretRef:
    name: example-app-git-auth
```

Token instead of a key (GitHub PAT, GitLab deploy token or project access
token), when the host makes tokens easier to scope than keys:

```bash
flux create secret git example-app-git-auth \
  --namespace=flux-system \
  --url=https://github.com/<owner>/example-app \
  --username=git \
  --password=<TOKEN>
```

The `url:` on the GitRepository then stays `https://…`, with the same
`secretRef.name`. Either way, rotating the credential is re-running the command;
nothing in git changes.

## Example

The comments in the file below stay within the three-line comment rule, so a
copy passes `task lint:comment-length`. The longer reasons live here:

- **No platform-store label.** The Namespace deliberately omits
  `{{ internal_domain }}/cluster-secrets: "true"`. That label is what the
  platform `ClusterSecretStore`'s `spec.conditions` admit, and it would give
  the tenant read access to every item in the platform vault. The tenant gets
  its own store instead.
- **Per-tenant vault.** Each tenant gets its own 1Password vault, named after
  the tenant, and a Connect token scoped to that vault alone, so a compromised
  tenant namespace cannot read platform items. The token Secret is bootstrap
  state (not Flux-managed) and scoped to this tenant, so revoking it is
  independent of every other tenant. Creating it and rotating it are the same
  commands:

  ```bash
  op vault create example-app
  op connect vault grant --server <EXISTING_SERVER_ID> --vault example-app
  op connect token create {{ cluster_name }}-example-app-eso \
    --server <EXISTING_SERVER_ID> --vaults example-app
  kubectl -n example-app create secret generic onepassword-connect-token \
    --from-literal=token=<CONNECT_TOKEN> --dry-run=client -o yaml \
    | kubectl apply -f -
  ```

  The Secret has no controller owner, so the unmanaged-secrets gate must
  allowlist `example-app/onepassword-connect-token` in the same change as the
  tenant file, and lose the entry when the tenant goes.
- **Private repository.** Add `secretRef: {name: example-app-git-auth}` to the
  GitRepository and create that Secret with `flux create secret git`, as in
  "Private tenant repositories" above. It is bootstrap state and lives in
  `flux-system`, alongside the GitRepository, because `secretRef` is
  namespace-local.
- **Hostname grant.** `tenant-routing-example-app` lists the exact hostnames
  the tenant may route, one per line; the platform decides them, never the
  tenant. A `*` anywhere in the grant makes both policies refuse every write
  they cover in the namespace, and the isolation gate refuses the grant, so a
  wildcard can never let one Ingress rule route many names. `maxPriority`, when
  set, is a plain ASCII whole number from 0 to 2147483647. The two
  bindings beside it apply `tenant-traefik-hostnames` and
  `tenant-certificates` to the tenant namespace alone, with that ConfigMap as
  their parameter. Every route must open with
  `Host()` on a granted hostname and may not use `||`, `HostRegexp` or any
  other `Host`; split an OR into one route per hostname. A route may set
  `priority` only when the grant carries `maxPriority`, and at most that, and
  may list only granted names in `tls.domains`. An Ingress needs a granted
  `host` on every rule, granted TLS hosts and
  `router.tls.domains.*` annotations, and no default backend. Traefik builds an
  Ingress router's rule from `spec.rules`, so the `router.rule`,
  `router.priority` and `router.rulesyntax` annotations, which would reshape or
  outrank it, are refused in any letter case. Of Traefik v3's other `router.*`
  annotations, `entrypoints`, `pathmatcher`, `middlewares` (held to the
  allowlist below), `tls`, `tls.certresolver`, `tls.options` and
  `observability.*` stay open: none changes the host the rule matches or its
  priority. Grant a hostname no platform route serves. A Certificate may name
  only granted hostnames in `dnsNames` and `commonName`, no wildcard, IP, URI,
  email, otherName or `literalSubject`, and may use only the platform
  ClusterIssuers (`letsencrypt-prod`, `letsencrypt-staging`) or an Issuer in
  its own namespace. The tenant does not write CertificateRequests to those
  ClusterIssuers itself: cert-manager creates them from the Certificate. A
  tenant-written CertificateRequest may name only a `cert-manager.io` Issuer
  in its own namespace; no external issuer kind is open to it.
- **Impersonation.** Without `serviceAccountName`, kustomize-controller applies
  tenant manifests with its own cluster-admin credentials. The ServiceAccount
  must live in the Kustomization's own namespace (`flux-system`), because
  kustomize-controller impersonates
  `system:serviceaccount:<Kustomization namespace>:<name>`. The RoleBindings
  then grant that ServiceAccount rights in the tenant namespace and nowhere
  else.

```yaml
# {{ cluster_name }}/tenants/example-app.yaml
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: example-app
  annotations:
    # Never pruned: a pruned Namespace takes every tenant object and PVC with
    # it, which the db Kustomization's `deletionPolicy: Orphan` cannot stop.
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: flux
    fluxcd.io/tenant: example-app
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: baseline
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    # DELIBERATELY ABSENT: {{ internal_domain }}/cluster-secrets: "true" would
    # admit this tenant to the platform store and its whole vault. The tenant
    # gets its own store, below (see "No platform-store label" in the README).
---
# Cap the namespace: `admin` places no ceiling on what a tenant may request.
# `pods:` must exceed the tenant's replica count, which surges by one on
# rollout, and `limits.memory` must cover that many per-pod memory limits.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: example-app-quota
  namespace: example-app
  annotations:
    # Kept with the Namespace, which is never pruned (see Removal above).
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "10"
    requests.cpu: "2"
    requests.memory: 4Gi
    limits.memory: 8Gi
{%- if tenant_storage %}
    # tenant-local sizes are not enforced; this bounds declared requests only.
    # Use "0" for a tenant that runs no database.
    persistentvolumeclaims: "4"
    tenant-local.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: 40Gi
{%- endif %}
---
# Defaults and per-container ceilings, so a pod with no resources block cannot
# land unbounded inside the quota. A `max:` key with no matching `default:` key
# becomes the default limit, so CPU is left to the quota's requests.cpu.
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: example-app-limits
  namespace: example-app
  annotations:
    # Kept with the Namespace, which is never pruned (see Removal above).
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      default:
        memory: 512Mi
      defaultRequest:
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 128Mi
      max:
        memory: 2Gi
---
# The tenant's own 1Password vault and a Connect token granted that vault
# alone. Bootstrap the token Secret and allowlist it as "Per-tenant vault" in
# the tenants README describes; the same commands rotate it.
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata:
  name: onepassword-example-app
spec:
  # Scoped to this tenant's namespace, so the NEXT tenant cannot name this store
  # and read through it. A ClusterSecretStore without conditions is usable from
  # every namespace in the cluster.
  conditions:
    - namespaces:
        - example-app
  provider:
    onepassword:
      # The cluster's 1Password Connect Service; change it if yours differs.
      connectHost: http://onepassword-connect.external-secrets.svc.cluster.local:8080
      # The tenant's own vault, never the platform vault.
      vaults:
        example-app: 1
      auth:
        secretRef:
          connectTokenSecretRef:
            name: onepassword-connect-token
            namespace: example-app
            key: token
# This is the 1Password shape. A tenant generated by weisssrv-app-template picks
# its own `secrets_backend`, and its docs/ONBOARDING.md step O1 is the
# authoritative wiring for whichever it chose - follow that file, not this block.
---
apiVersion: source.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: GitRepository
metadata:
  name: example-app
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 1m
  # This host because the example tenant is co-located; a tenant on GitHub (or
  # anywhere else Flux can clone from) writes its own URL here — see
  # "Tenant repositories are host-independent" above.
  url: https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/example-app
  ref:
    branch: main
  # Private repo: add `secretRef: {name: example-app-git-auth}` and create
  # that Secret in flux-system with `flux create secret git` — see "Private
  # repository" in the tenants README.
---
# The hostnames this tenant may route, one per line, granted by the platform.
# It lives in flux-system so the tenant cannot edit its own grant ("Hostname
# grant" in the tenants README).
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: tenant-routing-example-app
  namespace: flux-system
data:
  hosts: |
    example-app.{{ external_domain }}
    example-app.{{ internal_domain }}
---
# Holds this namespace's IngressRoutes and Ingresses to the grant above, and
# refuses them all if the grant is missing.
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: tenant-traefik-hostnames-example-app
spec:
  policyName: tenant-traefik-hostnames
  validationActions: [Deny]
  paramRef:
    name: tenant-routing-example-app
    namespace: flux-system
    parameterNotFoundAction: Deny
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: example-app
---
# Holds this namespace's cert-manager Certificates to the same grant, from a
# platform ClusterIssuer or the tenant's own Issuer.
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
  name: tenant-certificates-example-app
spec:
  policyName: tenant-certificates
  validationActions: [Deny]
  paramRef:
    name: tenant-routing-example-app
    namespace: flux-system
    parameterNotFoundAction: Deny
  matchResources:
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        kubernetes.io/metadata.name: example-app
---
# kustomize-controller impersonates this SA instead of using cluster-admin, so
# it lives in the Kustomization's own namespace (flux-system); the RoleBindings
# grant it rights in the tenant namespace only (README: "Impersonation").
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: example-app-flux
  namespace: flux-system
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: example-app-flux-admin
  namespace: example-app
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: example-app-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
# Fills admin's CRD gaps — see tenant-crd-editor.yaml.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: example-app-flux-crd-editor
  namespace: example-app
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: example-app-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: tenant-crd-editor
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: example-app
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 10m
  retryInterval: 1m
  timeout: 10m
  # Mirror the platform's apps.yaml: wait for the CRD chain so a fresh
  # bootstrap does not apply tenant CRs before ESO/cert-manager/Traefik exist.
  # The name must be this cluster's platform stage; a mismatch retries forever.
  dependsOn:
    - name: infrastructure-configs
  serviceAccountName: example-app-flux
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: example-app
  path: ./kubernetes/flux
  prune: true
  targetNamespace: example-app
  wait: true
  postBuild:
    substituteFrom:
      - kind: ConfigMap
        name: cluster-versions
        optional: false
      - kind: ConfigMap
        name: cluster-config
        optional: false
{%- if postgres_operator %}
---
# The database tree, for a tenant that runs one (docs/postgres.md).
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: example-app-db
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 10m
  retryInterval: 1m
  timeout: 10m
  dependsOn:
    - name: infrastructure-configs
  serviceAccountName: example-app-flux
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: example-app
  path: ./kubernetes/db
  prune: true
  # Flux >= 2.3. Deleting the wiring must never half-dismantle a database: the
  # default prunes its policies, ExternalSecrets and ScheduledBackup while the
  # prune-guarded Cluster runs on isolated, no longer archiving. Orphan keeps all.
  deletionPolicy: Orphan
  targetNamespace: example-app
  wait: true
  postBuild:
    substituteFrom:
      - kind: ConfigMap
        name: cluster-config
        optional: false
{%- endif %}
```

## Before onboarding the first tenant

- **Never label a tenant namespace into the platform store.** The platform
  `ClusterSecretStore` (`infrastructure/configs/cluster-secret-store.yaml`)
  carries `spec.conditions` admitting only namespaces labelled
  `{{ internal_domain }}/cluster-secrets: "true"`. That condition is the only
  thing standing between a tenant and the CI runner tokens, the identity
  provider's keys and the SMTP credentials — the tenant's `admin` RoleBinding
  lets it create ExternalSecrets freely, and RBAC in its own namespace cannot
  re-impose the boundary. Adding that label to a tenant namespace hands over the
  whole platform vault in one line. Give the tenant its own store instead, as
  the example above does.
- **Vault isolation.** Even a tenant's *own* `ClusterSecretStore` can read every
  item in whatever vault it points at, so a per-tenant store over the shared
  vault only isolates the token, not the contents. Give each tenant its own
  vault, named after the tenant, and a Connect token granted that vault alone
  (`op connect vault grant`, then `op connect token create --vaults <tenant>`),
  as the example above does, so a compromised tenant namespace cannot read
  platform items. Their store also needs its own `spec.conditions` — scope it
  to the tenant namespace, or the next tenant can name it too.
  `scripts/check-tenant-wiring.py` is the gate for a tenant store: the corpus
  gates read only the platform stage paths and never reach this tree. All of
  this applies to every backend, not only 1Password.
- **Merge the wiring only once the tenant repo carries a real image tag.** A
  placeholder tag no registry has leaves the Deployment at zero available
  replicas, and the tenant's own replica-availability alert fires critical in
  this cluster's Alertmanager minutes later.
- **Tenant storage is yours to back up.** A volume provisioned for a tenant
  namespace sits outside whatever backup set this cluster runs until you add
  the `{{ storage_backend }}`-backed volume behind the PV to it. Its snapshot
  schedule is the operator's, not the tenant's; `docs/RUNBOOKS.md` holds the
  backup and restore procedures.
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
- **The Connect Service the example names.** `onepassword-connect` in namespace
  `external-secrets`, on port 8080, is what a tenant store's `connectHost`
  resolves to. It ships with this cluster; a tenant pointed at its own Connect
  server writes that host instead.
{%- endif %}
- **Quota the namespace.** `admin` places no ceiling on requests, so an uncapped
  tenant namespace can starve the cluster. The wiring file above ships a
  `ResourceQuota` and a `LimitRange`; size both per tenant rather than copying
  the starting numbers. `pods:` has to exceed the tenant's replica count, which
  surges by one during a rollout, and `limits.memory` has to cover that many
  per-pod memory limits, or the surge pod is rejected and the rollout stalls.
- **Metrics must not ride the public port.** A tenant route matches on hostname,
  not path, so a `/metrics` endpoint served on the port the tenant's own
  IngressRoute publishes is reachable from the internet. Require a separate
  metrics `containerPort` and Service port, or a path rule on the route, at
  onboarding review.
- **A tenant PrometheusRule must scope its own expressions.** Prometheus runs
  without `enforcedNamespaceLabel`, deliberately, because the platform's own
  rules span namespaces. A tenant `expr` selecting only on `deployment="x"`
  therefore evaluates cluster-wide: it mis-fires on any like-named workload,
  and it silently disables an `absent()` arm as soon as another namespace has a
  match. Require `namespace="<slug>"` in the selector, not only in the alert
  labels.
- **Hostnames are granted by the platform.** Decide the tenant's hostnames at
  onboarding review and write them into its `tenant-routing-<ns>` ConfigMap,
  with the per-tenant bindings of `tenant-traefik-hostnames` and
  `tenant-certificates`, as the example does. A namespace whose grant is
  missing refuses every IngressRoute, Ingress and Certificate, and
  `task lint:tenant-traefik-isolation` fails without the grant and both
  bindings. Never grant a hostname a
  platform route serves.
- **Add the tenant's hostnames to the blackbox targets.** A tenant's own
  `PrometheusRule` covers replica availability and certificate readiness. A
  broken IngressRoute or DNS record while the replicas stay ready is caught only
  by an end-to-end probe, so add each hostname to the `targets:` list in
  `infrastructure/observability/exporters/blackbox-exporter.yaml` — `http_sso`
  for an SSO-gated route, `http_2xx` otherwise, and the internal hostname too
  where the tenant carries a second route.
- **Traefik cross-namespace refs.** The platform runs the Traefik CRD provider
  with `allowCrossNamespace: true` so app IngressRoutes can use the shared
  middlewares in the `traefik` namespace, and with
  `defaultTLSResourcesNamespace: traefik` so it ignores a `default` TLSOption
  or TLSStore defined anywhere else.
  `infrastructure/configs/tenant-traefik-policy.yaml` holds tenants to their own
  namespace: its ValidatingAdmissionPolicies, Deny-bound on every
  `fluxcd.io/tenant` namespace, pin each Service, Middleware, TLS and
  TraefikService reference to the tenant's namespace apart from the platform
  middlewares they allowlist, and refuse `name@provider` references. On a plain
  Ingress or Service, a `traefik.ingress.kubernetes.io/*` annotation may use
  `name@provider` only for an allowlisted platform middleware. Add any shared
  middleware a tenant needs to both allowlists. A tenant `forwardAuth`
  middleware may call only the platform's Authentik outpost or a Service in its
  own namespace. Traefik makes that call, and every route's backend call, with
  its own egress, which is wider than a tenant's; the policies pin what a tenant
  can point it at, not where Traefik itself may connect. A same-namespace
  Service name is only safe while that Service resolves to the tenant's own
  pods, so `tenant-no-externalname-services` refuses an ExternalName Service
  (it would CNAME Traefik into another namespace) and a Service with no
  selector. The same policy admits only internal ClusterIP Services, headless
  ones included, with no `externalIPs`: a NodePort or LoadBalancer Service
  reaches the tenant's pods through kube-proxy or a MetalLB VIP without
  Traefik, and `externalIPs` let kube-proxy capture traffic for any address
  in the cluster (the CVE-2020-8554 class). `tenant-no-manual-endpoints` refuses any Endpoints or
  EndpointSlice not written by the control plane (hand-written ones could name
  another namespace's pod or Service IP). An off-cluster backend a tenant needs
  is fronted from the platform `vm-ingress` namespace instead. What a route
  matches is pinned too: `tenant-traefik-hostnames` holds every IngressRoute
  and Ingress to the hostnames granted in the tenant's `tenant-routing-<ns>`
  ConfigMap, with no raised priority unless granted ("Hostname grant" above),
  and `tenant-no-tcp-udp-routes` refuses IngressRouteTCP and IngressRouteUDP,
  whose catch-all `HostSNI` or whole-port routers would take platform traffic.
  `tenant-traefik-tls-defaults` refuses a tenant TLSOption or TLSStore named
  `default`: Traefik's CRD provider drops a `default` defined in two namespaces,
  which would return every platform route to Traefik's built-in TLS defaults,
  and a lone tenant `default` TLSStore would set every route's default
  certificate. Certificates are issued only for granted hostnames:
  the ClusterIssuers solve DNS-01 for the whole zone, so `tenant-certificates`
  holds every tenant Certificate to the same grant as its routes. A raw
  CertificateRequest would skip that check, and tenants can write one: every
  tenant Flux ServiceAccount is bound to the built-in `admin` ClusterRole, and
  the cert-manager chart's default `global.rbac.aggregateClusterRoles: true`
  aggregates `cert-manager-edit`, which carries create and update on
  `certificaterequests` and `issuers`, into `admin`. cert-manager's internal
  approver approves every request, and the names sit inside the CSR PEM, which
  admission cannot read. So `tenant-certificaterequests` refuses any tenant
  CertificateRequest to an issuer other than a core `cert-manager.io` Issuer
  in the tenant's own namespace unless cert-manager's controller
  (`system:serviceaccount:cert-manager:cert-manager`, the chart's
  `serviceAccountName` for the `cert-manager` release) writes it from a
  Certificate `tenant-certificates` has already admitted. That covers the
  platform ClusterIssuers and any kind an external issuer plugin adds, such as
  `AWSPCAClusterIssuer`, which would sign outside the grant; an empty kind or
  group counts as the core Issuer, as cert-manager reads it. A Certificate is
  held to the same issuers: a platform ClusterIssuer or a core Issuer in its
  own namespace, nothing else. The same policy admits
  only that controller to create an ACME Order or Challenge
  (`acme.cert-manager.io`) in a tenant namespace. That is defence in depth,
  not a live bypass: `cert-manager-edit` grants no create or update on
  `orders`, only delete, and the policy holds if a future RBAC change widens
  that. The policy matches CREATE alone on CertificateRequests and Challenges,
  because cert-manager's validating webhook (`failurePolicy: Fail`) refuses
  any spec change to either after creation
  (`internal/apis/certmanager/validation/certificaterequest.go` and
  `internal/apis/acme/validation/challenge.go` in v1.21.2). An Order's spec is
  frozen only in `spec.request` (`internal/apis/acme/validation/order.go`), so
  Order UPDATE stays matched, with the garbage collector
  (`system:serviceaccount:kube-system:generic-garbage-collector`) admitted
  beside the controller for UPDATE only, so that removing a
  `foregroundDeletion` finalizer cannot wedge a deletion; ACME Orders and
  Challenges are created by cert-manager's controller only.
  Setting
  `global.rbac.aggregateClusterRoles: false` on the cert-manager release, and
  granting tenants Certificate and Issuer rights explicitly, would close the
  same path by authorization instead; the platform keeps the chart default and
  the policy. In full, the set is ten: `tenant-traefik-route-refs`,
  `tenant-traefik-object-refs`, `tenant-traefik-ingress-refs`,
  `tenant-traefik-hostnames`, `tenant-certificates`,
  `tenant-certificaterequests`, `tenant-no-tcp-udp-routes`,
  `tenant-traefik-tls-defaults`, `tenant-no-externalname-services` and
  `tenant-no-manual-endpoints`.
  Once any tenant is onboarded, `task lint:tenant-traefik-isolation` fails if
  the policies or their bindings go missing, if a binding narrows its match
  below every tenant namespace, or if a policy fails open, narrows its resources,
  or changes, drops or adds a variable or validation expression, even with
  `allowCrossNamespace: false`. It also fails unless each tenant namespace has
  its own Deny bindings of `tenant-traefik-hostnames` and
  `tenant-certificates`, both reading `flux-system/tenant-routing-<ns>` with
  `parameterNotFoundAction: Deny`, and
  that ConfigMap grants at least one hostname. The gate carries each
  expression verbatim, so a deliberate policy edit changes the gate's table in
  the same commit.
- **What remains cooperative in routing.** Four things the policies do not
  enforce. The grant itself: the platform must not list a hostname a platform
  route serves. The external-dns annotations on a tenant route
  (`external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/hostname`, `target`): external-dns's TXT
  ownership keeps a tenant from taking over an existing record, not from
  publishing a new name, so review them at onboarding. Routes written before
  the policies applied, until the audit below lists them and they are fixed.
  And what a route serves on its own hostnames, such as `/metrics` (above).
- **Audit what predates the policies.** The policies act on CREATE and UPDATE
  only, so an object written before they were applied, or into a namespace
  before it carried the `fluxcd.io/tenant` label, stays in place. Run
  `scripts/audit-tenant-admission.sh` (formerly `audit-tenant-services.sh`;
  needs `kubectl` against this cluster and `jq`) once at rollout and before
  each onboarding. It covers every kind the policies cover and lists, in every
  `fluxcd.io/tenant` namespace: ExternalName, NodePort and LoadBalancer
  Services, Services with `externalIPs`, selectorless Services,
  EndpointSlices not managed by `endpointslice-controller.k8s.io`, and
  Endpoints with no selector Service; IngressRoutes and Ingresses whose hosts
  are not all in the namespace's `flux-system/tenant-routing-<ns>` grant, or
  that carry a refused annotation, a `name@provider` or cross-namespace
  reference, `HostRegexp` or a priority above the grant; Middlewares whose
  forwardAuth address is neither a Service in the namespace nor the platform
  outpost; TraefikServices with cross-namespace or `@` references; any
  IngressRouteTCP or IngressRouteUDP; Certificates whose names are not all
  granted or whose issuer is not a platform ClusterIssuer or a core Issuer;
  tenant-written CertificateRequests to anything but a core Issuer; and a
  TLSOption or TLSStore named `default`. A missing or wildcard grant is one
  line per route, Ingress and Certificate. It exits non-zero while any remain.
  It does not trust labels, which a tenant can write: every EndpointSlice must
  name an existing selector Service through `kubernetes.io/service-name`, and
  every EndpointSlice and Endpoints address must be the IP of a live (not
  Succeeded or Failed) Pod in the same namespace that the Service's selector
  picks.
  Fix each one before the tenant's wiring merges. A cluster that takes the
  policies through `copier update` follows the migration in the template's
  [VERSIONING.md § Upgrade notes](https://github.com/ericsweiss/weisssrv-cluster-template/blob/main/docs/VERSIONING.md#upgrade-notes):
  the audit, then each tenant's hostname grant and binding before its routes
  reconcile, and the list of what tenants can no longer create.
- **Namespace ownership** is cooperative: nothing stops a tenant manifest from
  targeting a platform namespace today. An admission policy engine
  (Kyverno/OPA) is the durable fix.
